<section class="tc-hero"><div class="tc-w tc-hero-g"><div class="tc-hero-t"><p class="tc-eye"><span></span>Fit &middot; Accuracy &middot; Everyday wear</p><h1>Every second,<br><em>explained.</em></h1><p class="tc-lead">Tickers is an independent watch site. We help you pick a case size that suits your wrist, understand how accurate a watch should be and read the specs that actually matter.</p><p class="tc-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Use the watch tools</a><a class="tc-lk" href="case-size-guide.html">Read the size guide &rarr;</a></p></div><div class="tc-hero-v"><div class="tc-dial" aria-hidden="true"></div><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1773755060442-6d128b70dd20?auto=format&fit=crop&w=620&h=620&q=75" alt="close-up of a chronograph wristwatch" width="620" height="620" fetchpriority="high"></div></div>
<div class="tc-w tc-strip"><div><b>34&ndash;44</b><span>mm case range we cover</span></div><div><b>&plusmn;s/d</b><span>accuracy, in plain words</span></div><div><b>3</b><span>free calculators</span></div><div><b>0</b><span>watches for sale</span></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-s"><div class="tc-w"><div class="tc-h2"><span>01</span><h2>Three numbers to read on any watch</h2></div><div class="tc-three"><article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1524226210521-77ff136cba2d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=480&h=300&q=75" alt="person wearing a black watch" width="480" height="300" loading="lazy"><h3>Case diameter &amp; lug-to-lug</h3><p>Diameter is the headline number, but lug-to-lug length decides whether a watch sits on your wrist or hangs over the edges.</p></article><article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1623998021450-85c29c644e0d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=480&h=300&q=75" alt="gold and black round analog watch" width="480" height="300" loading="lazy"><h3>Accuracy in seconds per day</h3><p>Written as &plusmn;s/d. Mechanical movements vary with position, temperature and how fully wound they are.</p></article><article><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1589391943533-d6856910b7a8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=480&h=300&q=75" alt="black and silver chronograph watch" width="480" height="300" loading="lazy"><h3>Water resistance in metres</h3><p>A pressure test, not a diving depth. 30 m means splashes; 100 m means swimming; 200 m means water sport.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-tl" id="tools"><div class="tc-w"><div class="tc-h2"><span>02</span><h2>Watch tools</h2></div><div class="tc-tools">
<div class="tc-tool"><header><b>A</b><div><h3>Wrist Fit Checker</h3><p>Will this case suit your wrist?</p></div></header><div class="tc-f2"><label for="wf-w">Wrist (cm)<input id="wf-w" type="number" min="12" max="24" step="0.5" value="17"></label><label for="wf-d">Case diameter (mm)<input id="wf-d" type="number" min="26" max="50" step="1" value="40"></label><label for="wf-l">Lug-to-lug (mm)<input id="wf-l" type="number" min="30" max="60" step="1" value="47"></label></div><div class="tc-out" id="wf-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="tc-tool"><header><b>B</b><div><h3>Accuracy Tracker</h3><p>Turn a week of checks into seconds per day.</p></div></header><div class="tc-f2"><label for="ac-s">Seconds gained (use &minus; for lost)<input id="ac-s" type="number" step="1" value="21"></label><label for="ac-d">Over how many days<input id="ac-d" type="number" min="1" max="60" step="1" value="7"></label><label for="ac-t">Movement<select id="ac-t"><option value="mech">Mechanical / automatic</option><option value="chron">Chronometer certified</option><option value="quartz">Quartz</option></select></label></div><div class="tc-out" id="ac-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="tc-tool"><header><b>C</b><div><h3>Water Resistance Decoder</h3><p>What the rating on the dial really allows.</p></div></header><div class="tc-f2"><label for="wr-r">Rating on the watch<select id="wr-r"><option value="0">None / not stated</option><option value="30">30 m / 3 bar</option><option value="50">50 m / 5 bar</option><option value="100" selected>100 m / 10 bar</option><option value="200">200 m / 20 bar</option><option value="300">300 m and above / dive</option></select></label><label for="wr-c">Crown type<select id="wr-c"><option value="push">Push-pull crown</option><option value="screw">Screw-down crown</option></select></label></div><div class="tc-out" id="wr-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="tc-s"><div class="tc-w tc-split"><div><div class="tc-h2"><span>03</span><h2>A daily routine for a mechanical watch</h2></div><ol class="tc-tml"><li><time>07:00</time><p><b>Check the time</b> against a reliable clock and note the difference.</p></li><li><time>07:01</time><p><b>Wind if needed.</b> Twenty to thirty turns of the crown for a manual watch that has stopped.</p></li><li><time>All day</time><p><b>Keep it away from magnets</b>: phone cases, speakers and bag clasps.</p></li><li><time>22:00</time><p><b>Rest it crown-up</b> if it runs slow, dial-up if it runs fast. Position changes the rate.</p></li><li><time>Weekly</time><p><b>Wipe the case and bracelet</b> with a soft cloth; rinse in fresh water after salt water if it is rated for it.</p></li></ol></div><figure class="tc-split-i"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1519982790242-9f401cbfbc84?auto=format&fit=crop&w=560&h=640&q=75" alt="person checking the time on a white dial watch" width="560" height="640" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="tc-band"><div class="tc-w tc-band-g"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1708453481658-117ced2f1d12?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&h=420&q=75" alt="black and white photo of a watch" width="600" height="420" loading="lazy"><div><p class="tc-eye"><span></span>Our approach</p><h2>We don&rsquo;t sell watches. We explain them.</h2><p>Tickers is written by people who wear and test watches, then checks every figure against published specifications. You will not find shop links, sponsored posts or paid rankings here.</p><a class="tc-lk" href="about.html">How we work &rarr;</a></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-s"><div class="tc-w"><div class="tc-h2"><span>04</span><h2>The guides</h2></div><div class="tc-gds"><a href="case-size-guide.html"><div class="tc-gi"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1667376912105-85fe11a8845f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="close up of a watch" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"></div><div class="tc-gt"><small>Guide 01 &middot; 8 min</small><h3>The Case Size Guide</h3><p>Diameter, lug-to-lug, thickness and strap width, matched to your wrist measurement.</p></div></a><a href="watch-accuracy-guide.html"><div class="tc-gi"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1528595343328-b96a6cdf6e42?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="round black analog watch" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"></div><div class="tc-gt"><small>Guide 02 &middot; 8 min</small><h3>The Watch Accuracy Guide</h3><p>What &plusmn;s/d means, why watches drift and when a watchmaker should take a look.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="tc-s tc-dark"><div class="tc-w tc-fq"><div><div class="tc-h2"><span>05</span><h2>Questions we hear most</h2></div><p class="tc-fq-n">Still unsure? <a href="contact.html">Send us your question</a> and we will answer it.</p></div><div class="tc-faq"><details open><summary>What watch size suits my wrist?</summary><p>Measure your wrist with a strip of paper. As a rough guide, wrists under 16 cm suit cases of about 34 to 38 mm, 16 to 18 cm suit 38 to 41 mm, and larger wrists can wear 41 mm and up. Lug-to-lug length matters as much as diameter.</p></details><details><summary>How accurate should a mechanical watch be?</summary><p>Many everyday automatic movements run within about &plusmn;10 to 20 seconds a day. Chronometer-certified movements are tested to tighter limits. Quartz watches typically drift only a few seconds a month.</p></details><details><summary>Why does my watch gain time?</summary><p>The most common causes are magnetism from phones, speakers or bag clasps, a recent knock, or simply the movement&rsquo;s position overnight. A watchmaker can test and demagnetise it quickly.</p></details><details><summary>Does &ldquo;30 m water resistant&rdquo; mean I can swim with it?</summary><p>Usually not. That rating generally means splash resistant only. For swimming, look for at least 100 m, and for regular water sport, 200 m with a screw-down crown.</p></details><details><summary>How often should a watch be serviced?</summary><p>Many makers suggest every five to ten years for a mechanical watch, or sooner if it starts losing a lot of time or the power reserve drops. Water resistance seals are worth checking every year or two.</p></details><details><summary>What is lug-to-lug length?</summary><p>The distance from the tip of the top lugs to the tip of the bottom lugs. If it is longer than the flat top of your wrist, the watch will overhang and look too big, whatever the diameter.</p></details></div></div></section>

